  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/Sanish V vsFoodInspector on 7 August, 2020.json",
  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sanish V vs Food Inspector on 7 August, 2020 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr.Justice N.Anil Kumar Friday, The 07Th Day Of August 2020 / 16Th Sravana, 1942 Crl.Rev.Pet.No.1933 Of 2011 Against The Judgment In Crl Appeal No.614/2009 Dated 07-07-2011 Of Additional District And Sessions Court Fast Track(Adho-Ii), Kozhikode Against The Judgment In St 1132/2007 Dated 19-09-2009 Of Judicial Magistrate Of First Class ,Kunnamangalam Revision Petitioners/Appellant/Accused: 1 Sanish V., S/O. Kunjikannan, Aged 30 Year, Vettuvadakkayil House, Thadiangode P./O., Kozhikode. 2 N.Sreedharan Nair, S/O. Narayanan Nair Aged 64 Years, Residing At Sauparnika, Palluvila, Chellamangalam P.O., Thiruvananthapuram. 3 Ms. White Filed Dairy Pvt. Ltd Sundarapuri, Pollachi, Tamil Nadu. By Advs. Sri.Santhosh Subramanian Sri.Sherry J. Thomas Sri.S.Sreekumar(North Paravur) Sri.E.A.Thankappan Respondents/Respondents/Complainant: 1 Food Inspector, Kunnamangalam Circle, Kunnamangalam, Kozhikode, Pin-673 001. 2 State Of Kerala Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala,Ernakulam-31. Sr.Pp. M.S.Breez Sanish V vs Food Inspector on 7 August, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111180757/ 1 This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 30-07-2020, The Court On 07-08-2020 Passed The Following: Crl.Rev.Pet.No.1933 Of 2011 2 Order",
    "facts": "Being aggrieved, the accused 1 to 3 preferred this revision. 3. The prosecution cases in brief, is that on 29.03.2007, the Food Inspector, Kunnamangalam Circle inspected a milk- sales van bearing No.KL-07-8471 on 29.03.2007 and purchased three packets of pasteurized tonned milk containing 500ml each from the stock of 15 packets kept inside the plastic tray on payment of Rs.22.50/- After complying with all legal formalities for sampling, one sample was sent to the public analyst for report. The public analyst submitted his report stating that upon analysis of the sample of milk, the sample does not conform the prescribed standard for tonned milk. The said sample was therefore adulterated within the meaning of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act and the Rules framed thereunder. 4. Based upon the report of the public analyst, the learned Magistrate took cognizance of the offence and issued process against accused 1 to 3. The public analyst by Form III report opined that the sample is not conforming to the standard prescribed for tonned milk and as per Ext.P13, milk fat was noted as 2.9, and as per the standard, it shall not be less than Crl.Rev.Pet.No.1933 OF 2011 3%, and Solids Not Fat (SNF) was noted as 7.6% and as per the standard, it shall not be less than 8.5%. 5. Being aggrieved by the finding in Ext.P13, another part of the sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory under Section 13(2) of the Food Adulteration Act and Ext.P26 Form II certificate was obtained. Ext.P26 Form II certificate would show that the milk fat as 7.6% (as per standard it shall not be less than 3%) and Solid Not Fat (SNF) noted as 5.15% (as per the Act not less than 8.5%). Sanish V vs Food Inspector on 7 August, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/111180757/ 2 The learned counsel for the revision petitioners filed this criminal revision petition before this court as early as on 09.08.2011.",
    "arguments": "One of the main grounds taken by the learned counsel for the revision petitioners in the revision petition is narrated as herein below: \"F) Mandatory provisions of law contained in Sections 23(1) (A) (ee) (hh) of the Act are violated in this case and the Laboratories in which Ext.P13 was issued was not a defined laboratory and those rules relating to the establishment of the laboratory were not laid before Crl.Rev.Pet.No.1933 OF 2011 parliament as required in Section 23(2) of the P.F.A. Act and Ext.P26 certificate was issued by the Laboratory and those labs are not defined laboratory and the methods followed are not laid before parliament and not published and defined and the general public and industry are not informed nor those methods are appended in the Act or Rule nor published in official gazettes and as such are secret method which are illegal and hence no legal sanctity can be given to Ext.P13 and P26 and they are liable to be rejected and dismissed by this Hon'ble Court and as prescribed in Rule 3(c) of the PFA Rules standarised methods of analysis are to be followed in all laboratories and the lack of defined laboratories as per law and lack of standarised methods of analysis and as laid before parliament and published and appended in the Rules and in the absence of such mandatory requirements of law the entire procedure were vitiated and Courts below had not considered those aspects.\" 8. In Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector and Another [2010 (4) KHC 767], the apex court held that in order to form a foundation for the prosecution after 01.04.1976, ie., the date of incorporation of the provisions under Section 23(1A) (ee), the report of the public analyst must be one based on the analysis from a laboratory defined under Section 23(1A) (ee) following the procedure provided under Section 23(1A) (hh). The decision of the apex court in Pepsico India Holdings's case is followed by a Division Bench of this Court in Raheed N.K. v. Food Inspector, Thalassery and Another [2016 (2) KHC 679].",
    "petitioner_arguments": "One of the main grounds taken by the learned counsel for the revision petitioners in the revision petition is narrated as herein below: \"F) Mandatory provisions of law contained in Sections 23(1) (A) (ee) (hh) of the Act are violated in this case and the Laboratories in which Ext.P13 was issued was not a defined laboratory and those rules relating to the establishment of the laboratory were not laid before Crl.Rev.Pet.No.1933 OF 2011 parliament as required in Section 23(2) of the P.F.A. Act and Ext.P26 certificate was issued by the Laboratory and those labs are not defined laboratory and the methods followed are not laid before parliament and not published and defined and the general public and industry are not informed nor those methods are appended in the Act or Rule nor published in official gazettes and as such are secret method which are illegal and hence no legal sanctity can be given to Ext.P13 and P26 and they are liable to be rejected and dismissed by this Hon'ble Court and as prescribed in Rule 3(c) of the PFA Rules standarised methods of analysis are to be followed in all laboratories and the lack of defined laboratories as per law and lack of standarised methods of analysis and as laid before parliament and published and appended in the Rules and in the absence of such mandatory requirements of law the entire procedure were vitiated and Courts below had not considered those aspects.\" 8.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In Pepsico India Holdings Pvt. Ltd. v. Food Inspector and Another [2010 (4) KHC 767], the apex court held that in order to form a foundation for the prosecution after 01.04.1976, ie., the date of incorporation of the provisions under Section 23(1A) (ee), the report of the public analyst must be one based on the analysis from a laboratory defined under Section 23(1A) (ee) following the procedure provided under Section 23(1A) (hh). The decision of the apex court in Pepsico India Holdings's case is followed by a Division Bench of this Court in Raheed N.K. v. Food Inspector, Thalassery and Another [2016 (2) KHC 679]."
  },
